<html xmlns:o='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office' xmlns:w='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40'>
<head>
<title>Security Assessment Report</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: 'Calibri', sans-serif; line-height: 1.6; }
  h1 { font-size: 24px; color: #2e74b5; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; padding-bottom: 5px;}
  h2 { font-size: 20px; color: #2e74b5; margin-top: 20px;}
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 20px;}
  th, td { border: 1px solid #aaaaaa; padding: 8px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;}
  th { background-color: #f2f2f2; font-weight: bold; }
  .status { font-weight: bold; }
  .status.pass { color: green; }
  .status.warn { color: #d09000; }
  .status.fail { color: red; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>Application Security Assessment Report</h1>
<p><strong>Date of Assessment</strong>: July 30, 2026<br/>
<strong>Scope</strong>: Codebase Security Review and Architecture Analysis<br/>
<strong>Application Context</strong>: PSNA College of Engineering &amp; Technology Admission Portal</p>

<h2>Executive Summary</h2>
<p>This document provides a comprehensive evaluation of the application's security posture, based on a direct review of its source code, architecture configurations, and deployment scripts. The assessment evaluates nine key security and compliance domains, highlighting implemented safeguards as well as areas requiring formalization.</p>

<h2>1. Audits and Professional Reviews</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th width='25%'>Security Control</th>
    <th width='20%'>Implementation Status</th>
    <th width='55%'>Detailed Findings</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Independent Cybersecurity Audit</strong></td>
    <td class='status warn'>&#9888; Not Documented</td>
    <td>The application's architecture includes internal security considerations (e.g., encryption, secure cookies). However, there is no formal report or certificate in the repository indicating a third-party independent cybersecurity audit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>VAPT (Vulnerability Assessment &amp; Penetration Testing)</strong></td>
    <td class='status warn'>&#9888; Not Documented</td>
    <td>No records, scan outputs, or VAPT assessment certificates are present in the codebase.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Cybersecurity Professional Review</strong></td>
    <td class='status warn'>&#9888; Not Documented</td>
    <td>While secure coding patterns (e.g., parameterized queries, Bcrypt, AES-256) are used, formal sign-off or pre-deployment review documentation by dedicated cybersecurity professionals is absent.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>2. Encryption and Data Protection</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th width='25%'>Security Control</th>
    <th width='20%'>Implementation Status</th>
    <th width='55%'>Detailed Findings</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Data at Rest Encryption</strong></td>
    <td class='status pass'>&#9989; Fully Compliant</td>
    <td>Sensitive student payloads are encrypted symmetrically using <strong>AES-256-GCM</strong> via the Node.js <code>crypto</code> module (256-bit key, 96-bit random IVs). The PostgreSQL database utilizes the <code>pgcrypto</code> extension for cryptographic operations.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Data in Transit Encryption</strong></td>
    <td class='status pass'>&#9989; Fully Compliant</td>
    <td>Traffic is secured via standard <strong>HTTPS/TLS 1.2+</strong>. Session cookies are hardened with <code>httpOnly: true</code>, <code>sameSite: 'lax'</code>, and <code>secure: true</code> (in production) to prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) and interception.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Password Storage Standards</strong></td>
    <td class='status pass'>&#9989; Fully Compliant</td>
    <td>Admin passwords are securely hashed using <strong>Bcrypt</strong> (<code>bcryptjs</code>). A work factor of <strong>10 salt rounds</strong> is utilized, which automatically generates unique salts and prevents brute-force/rainbow table attacks (OWASP Standard).</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>3. Authentication and Access Control</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th width='25%'>Security Control</th>
    <th width='20%'>Implementation Status</th>
    <th width='55%'>Detailed Findings</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Authentication Mechanisms</strong></td>
    <td class='status pass'>&#9989; Implemented</td>
    <td><strong>Admins</strong>: Username/Password validated against Bcrypt hashes.<br/><strong>Students</strong>: Application Number/Institutional ID + Date of Birth.<br/><strong>Sessions</strong>: Handled via cryptographically secure UUID tokens stored in the database.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)</strong></td>
    <td class='status fail'>&#10060; Missing</td>
    <td>MFA / 2FA is <strong>not implemented</strong> for privileged (admin) or student accounts.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</strong></td>
    <td class='status warn'>&#9888; Partially Implemented</td>
    <td>A 2-tier model separates <code>admin</code> and <code>student</code> roles using server-side session guards. However, granular admin privileges (e.g., Super Admin vs. Read-Only Admin) are not supported.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>4. Monitoring, Logging, and Operations</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th width='25%'>Security Control</th>
    <th width='20%'>Implementation Status</th>
    <th width='55%'>Detailed Findings</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Audit Logging</strong></td>
    <td class='status fail'>&#10060; Missing</td>
    <td>There is no dedicated audit log table to track critical administrative operations (e.g., bulk exports, deletions, record modifications).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Activity Monitoring</strong></td>
    <td class='status warn'>&#9888; Minimal</td>
    <td>Tracking is limited to session expirations and standard database timestamps (<code>created_at</code>, <code>updated_at</code>). There is no real-time threat monitoring or SIEM integration.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Backups and Disaster Recovery</strong></td>
    <td class='status warn'>&#9888; Partially Addressed</td>
    <td>Database backups inherently protect sensitive data due to AES-256-GCM encrypted payloads. However, automated DR runbooks, scheduled backups, and formal incident response procedures are not defined within the codebase itself.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Hosting Environment Compliance</strong></td>
    <td class='status pass'>&#9989; Standard Cloud</td>
    <td>Deployed on <strong>Cloudlets (Jelastic Cloud Platform)</strong> (<code>admissionspsna.in1.cloudlets.co.in</code>). Infrastructure compliance (ISO 27001 / SOC 2) is dependent on the vendor's data center capabilities.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div style='background-color: #f8f9fa; border-left: 4px solid #2e74b5; padding: 10px; margin-top: 20px;'>
  <strong>Conclusion &amp; Recommendations</strong><br/>
  The application is built on a solid foundation of standard security practices, particularly in its use of <strong>Bcrypt</strong> for password hashing and <strong>AES-256-GCM</strong> for data encryption. However, to meet stringent enterprise compliance standards, it is highly recommended to:
  <ol>
    <li>Conduct a formal <strong>VAPT</strong> and independent security audit.</li>
    <li>Implement <strong>MFA</strong> for administrative accounts.</li>
    <li>Introduce dedicated <strong>Audit Logging</strong> for all administrative actions.</li>
  </ol>
</div>
</body>
</html>
